00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:48.880
that's ongoing at the
Ensemble Studio of Theater

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:51.430
in New York City called What You Are Now.

00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:54.290
And it really is a culmination
and a representation

00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:56.520
of neuroscience meets the arts.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:59.233
And this actual play has so many topics

00:00:59.233 --> 00:01:02.820
that are at the heart of what
we talk about on this podcast.

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:04.080
So in this conversation today,

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:07.270
I'm honored to be able to
host the playwright Sam Chanse

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:10.820
as well as three cast
members from the production,

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:14.560
Pisay Pao, Robert Lee
Leng and Sonnie Brown.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:16.460
It's a great conversation and with that,

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:18.360
let's just go ahead and jump in.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:20.943
(upbeat music)

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:27.920
Well, welcome back to another
episode of the Addy Hour.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:30.590
It's great to be here with
all of you and as always,

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:33.410
I've really enjoyed
hosting these conversations

00:01:33.410 --> 00:01:35.720
and just having the opportunity
to peel back the layers

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:37.890
as we're on so many important topics

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:39.260
and to have so many different guests

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:40.420
from different walks of life

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:42.880
who've been able to share
their professional expertise

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:45.300
and their personal experiences as well.

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.020
It's been encouraging to hear how you all

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:48.360
as audience members and listeners

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:50.270
have really engaged in these topics.

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:52.210
And today I'm excited to be able to carry

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:54.130
that conversation forward again.

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:56.840
I had the opportunity to actually
see a preview of this play

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:57.820
a few weeks back,

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:01.470
and it's really powerful and
gripping story about memories,

00:02:01.470 --> 00:02:04.310
about trauma, about resilience and hope.

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:07.230
Centers on the family
and the story of family

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:10.100
of Cambodian refugees
here in the United States

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:13.340
and their journey also leans
into the lead character

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:14.860
who's actually a neuroscientist

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:16.690
researching memory formation,

00:02:16.690 --> 00:02:19.330
and also thinking about ways
that memory can be altered

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:20.760
and perhaps even removed

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:23.460
when thinking about the traumas
and challenges her family

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:24.900
has been through.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:25.733
So today, again,

00:02:25.733 --> 00:02:28.220
I'm honored to be able to be
able to host the playwright

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:29.400
and some of the cast members

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:32.060
for what I think will be a
really great conversation.

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:33.830
We're doing something a little
bit different because we have

00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:35.950
four guests for the first time.

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:37.230
Usually we have three or less,

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:38.220
but I think this conversation

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:39.970
is one that's really gonna flow well

00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:42.820
and also knowing the work
that they've done together

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:44.150
as a group, I'm really excited

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:46.160
to be able to host the conversation.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:49.330
So to start out, I'd like
to introduce Sam Chanse,

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:51.820
who is the author of the play.

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:54.120
She is someone who has
been deeply involved

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:55.770
in lots of different types of work.

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:58.110
I promise her I'd keep
the introduction short,

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:00.170
not to go on too long about the accolades

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:02.940
so I'll leave it there for
now, but we'll also come back

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:06.260
some of her investment in
this topic as we go through.

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:08.590
But first thing I wanted to
actually pass it on to Sam

00:03:08.590 --> 00:03:11.540
to introduce the cast members
who are here joining us today.

00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:13.190
So Sam, I'll pass it over to you.

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:18.080
- Sure, so I'm just going
in order of the Zoom boxes

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:20.366
as they've appear on my screen.

00:03:20.366 --> 00:03:23.930
Pisay Pao is here with us.

00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:25.450
You're all in New York.

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:27.070
I'm in California right now,

00:03:27.070 --> 00:03:32.070
but Pisay is playing the
role of Pia in the play.

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:36.827
Robert Lee Leng who plays
the role of the Ronnie

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:43.080
in the play and Sonnie Brown

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:46.603
who is playing the role of Shantrea.

00:03:46.603 --> 00:03:48.590
- And Sam, I actually wanted
to start out with you.

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:50.480
As my listeners know,

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:52.470
I often like to check
in and see how people

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:54.250
are doing at this moment in time.

00:03:54.250 --> 00:03:55.750
And with everything that we've

00:03:55.750 --> 00:03:56.930
walked through in the last few years,

00:03:56.930 --> 00:03:59.210
I know for the Ensemble Studio Theater,

00:03:59.210 --> 00:04:01.340
this was the first production in a while

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:04.560
that was actually done in
person with a live audience.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:05.750
So I just wanted to ask you

00:04:05.750 --> 00:04:07.850
what that experience has been like

00:04:07.850 --> 00:04:10.890
being back with the
production that you've written

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:13.380
in an in person setting,

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:15.490
how has that been over
these last few weeks

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:18.232
as the play has kicked on?

00:04:18.232 --> 00:04:23.232
- I mean for me, it's been
really amazing to be in the...

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:27.920
To have had the time
in the room with folks.

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:30.330
And I wasn't there for
the whole process because

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:33.535
I'm in California right now
so I haven't been able to...

00:04:33.535 --> 00:04:35.420
So I was joining on Zoom for a while,

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:38.780
which was familiar territory
after the last few years.

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:40.810
And I think it always
felt like this production

00:04:40.810 --> 00:04:42.200
was very uncertain.

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:46.150
I think everything has felt really fragile

00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:48.460
and I kind of didn't know...

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:51.050
I never really knew if
things were gonna proceed

00:04:51.050 --> 00:04:53.500
from week to week, like
what was going to happen and

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:55.390
would this ever have an audience?

00:04:55.390 --> 00:04:57.140
Would we ever be in the space together?

00:04:57.140 --> 00:05:01.430
And so being in the space with everyone

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:04.750
and working together in this has been

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:09.370
it's just really beautiful.

00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:12.850
And I think it really is uncertain also.

00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:14.180
I feel aware of that.

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:17.160
Like, it feels like
everyone's been really putting

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:20.140
a lot out there to be
there and risking a lot

00:05:20.140 --> 00:05:23.940
and after the last few
years, I think it just...

00:05:26.470 --> 00:05:29.380
I think there's a
different kind of awareness

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:31.823
of the preciousness of
being together in space.

00:05:34.010 --> 00:05:37.420
- That's really well said and
mirrors just a lot of things,

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:39.360
just I'm sure as you all have heard just

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:41.510
from so many folks too, that
have held those two together,

00:05:41.510 --> 00:05:43.190
I mean, having that
precious beautiful moment,

00:05:43.190 --> 00:05:46.550
but also the uncertainty
that's there at the same time.

00:05:46.550 --> 00:05:48.000
So definitely appreciate how you

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:51.653
honestly shared that and
still true in a lot of ways.

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:55.350
The only thing I wanted to
ask just to get a feel from

00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:56.490
the cast members as well,

00:05:56.490 --> 00:05:59.360
how has that been for you
all being back onstage

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:03.310
and having people in the
audience actually listening in

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:06.230
and engaging with you and then also,

00:06:06.230 --> 00:06:08.710
if you could actually just
talk about maybe a little bit

00:06:08.710 --> 00:06:11.670
about your journey and what drew you

00:06:11.670 --> 00:06:12.900
to this production as well.

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:14.970
I'd rather just ask
three questions in one,

00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:17.530
but I'm gonna let you all take from there.

00:06:17.530 --> 00:06:20.250
Actually, if we could start
with Pisay, that'd be great.

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:25.250
- Sure, I think I'm just sort
of still, what's the word...

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:31.840
What Sam just said really,

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:34.223
really touched me about the uncertainty.

00:06:35.260 --> 00:06:37.800
I think that she just hits something

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:42.310
that's sort of been inside me
for the last couple of months

00:06:42.310 --> 00:06:43.930
of working on this play,

00:06:43.930 --> 00:06:48.470
the uncertainty of whether
this play was gonna go on.

00:06:48.470 --> 00:06:51.710
We test every week so at
any point in the week,

00:06:51.710 --> 00:06:53.230
we could be shut down,

00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:55.920
whether we were going to have an audience.

00:06:55.920 --> 00:07:00.200
This is my first time on stage
so the uncertainty there.

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:04.030
So the weight of that is
just sort of hitting me

00:07:04.879 --> 00:07:06.840
at this very moment.

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:10.300
And it's really, really, I think,

00:07:10.300 --> 00:07:13.633
closely tied to being a refugee,

00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:16.950
the uncertainty that my parents felt

00:07:16.950 --> 00:07:20.723
and the uncertainty that I
felt growing up in general.

00:07:22.780 --> 00:07:25.940
And it's just all hitting me
right now at this very moment

00:07:25.940 --> 00:07:30.600
cause of that one word that
Sam said, so thank you, Sam,

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:34.903
for sharing that.

00:07:35.850 --> 00:07:39.590
I think that definitely
encapsulates my process

00:07:40.460 --> 00:07:42.410
moving forward, but not certain

00:07:42.410 --> 00:07:45.990
if we are going to actually go forward,

00:07:45.990 --> 00:07:48.350
but still continuing

00:07:48.350 --> 00:07:52.207
just like marching on because

00:07:55.020 --> 00:07:57.963
damned if I'm gonna get on
stage and not know my lines.

00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:04.460
So I don't know if that
answers all of your questions,

00:08:04.460 --> 00:08:09.030
but that's definitely where
I am at this very moment

00:08:09.030 --> 00:08:12.720
just having heard what Sam had to share.

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:13.920
- Thanks so much for sharing that

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:15.480
and just being in the moment too.

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:16.420
I mean, it sounds like a lot of it

00:08:16.420 --> 00:08:19.000
is just kind of hitting
you in a way all at once

00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:19.833
that it hadn't before.

00:08:19.833 --> 00:08:21.520
Would you say that did
you kind of sense it

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:23.840
as you were walking through?

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:24.760
Or was it also cause...

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:26.070
I mean, at some level,

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:28.620
I assume you're also trying
to be about your craft

00:08:28.620 --> 00:08:29.453
and about your work.

00:08:29.453 --> 00:08:31.890
So you think it's something
that you just were focused

00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:34.070
in the moment and at this
time have time to pause

00:08:34.070 --> 00:08:34.990
or do you think you kind of sensed it

00:08:34.990 --> 00:08:35.823
as you were moving

00:08:35.823 --> 00:08:38.370
through that kinda feeling of uncertainty?

00:08:38.370 --> 00:08:40.110
- I think I definitely sensed it,

00:08:40.110 --> 00:08:44.160
but I'm reading this book right now called

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:46.070
the Body Keeps the Score.

00:08:46.070 --> 00:08:49.430
And one of the main things about trauma

00:08:49.430 --> 00:08:51.500
is that you don't have...

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:53.940
That most people can't verbalize it.

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:57.360
They can't put word to
what they're experiencing.

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:02.100
So I've definitely sensed it
throughout this whole process,

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:05.890
but I didn't realize
that what I was sensing

00:09:05.890 --> 00:09:10.890
was uncertainty even though
I was feeling it sort of

00:09:11.990 --> 00:09:15.240
all throughout because of
all the different factors

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:18.750
that we were talking about
with COVID, especially,

00:09:18.750 --> 00:09:22.253
and with it being my
first experience so yes.

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:26.373
A thousand percent I sensed it.

00:09:27.387 --> 00:09:32.263
And when Sam put a word to it,
it just sort of like binged.

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:36.473
- That's really well said.

00:09:36.473 --> 00:09:39.840
Appreciate you sharing,
so honestly as well.

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:43.057
Sonnie what about you, how
has this process been for you?

00:09:45.590 --> 00:09:48.240
- It's been an amazing process.

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:51.590
I remember one of the early
rehearsals asking our director,

00:09:51.590 --> 00:09:54.080
Steve Kasum, is there a possibility

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:59.080
the show's not gonna go on
because we had the last surge

00:10:01.070 --> 00:10:03.870
of COVID and it was
affecting a lot of people

00:10:03.870 --> 00:10:05.720
and I was think, wow, okay.

00:10:05.720 --> 00:10:06.870
So what's going, I mean,

00:10:06.870 --> 00:10:08.853
are we here rehearsing for ourselves,

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:13.930
but even if we were just
rehearsing for ourselves,

00:10:13.930 --> 00:10:18.053
the play hit me in such a
way that it was worth it,

00:10:18.920 --> 00:10:22.550
because I've told Sam
this a couple of times,

00:10:22.550 --> 00:10:25.883
and I tell everybody this is
the best role I've ever had.

00:10:27.070 --> 00:10:30.360
Just in the complexity of the woman,

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:32.110
the things that she's gone through,

00:10:33.890 --> 00:10:37.100
that people can find humor in her

00:10:37.100 --> 00:10:42.100
and that she can break some
hearts is a dream role.

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:45.220
So I mean, it's amazing for me.

00:10:45.220 --> 00:10:50.140
So the whole process has
been incredibly fulfilling,

00:10:50.140 --> 00:10:51.880
but at the same time in the background,

00:10:51.880 --> 00:10:54.880
there's all this stuff
going on in the world

00:10:54.880 --> 00:10:57.300
and COVID is still a thing

00:10:57.300 --> 00:10:58.920
and we're still getting tested.

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:00.743
We had a couple of scares,

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:07.483
but I mean, I personally
don't only speak for myself.

00:11:08.450 --> 00:11:11.410
It's all been worth any kind of stress

00:11:11.410 --> 00:11:15.423
or uncertainty for me personally,

00:11:15.423 --> 00:11:19.170
it's just been a great journey.

00:11:19.170 --> 00:11:20.190
- That's really powerful.

00:11:20.190 --> 00:11:21.070
That's so great to hear.

00:11:21.070 --> 00:11:22.970
I mean, in a sense as you're both sharing,

00:11:22.970 --> 00:11:24.560
everything that you're
continuing to navigate,

00:11:24.560 --> 00:11:27.070
even about your work

00:11:27.070 --> 00:11:29.300
and putting this on for audiences as well

00:11:29.300 --> 00:11:34.300
so definitely appreciate that
full spectrum of emotions

00:11:34.570 --> 00:11:36.050
and feelings that have
kind of been going on

00:11:36.050 --> 00:11:37.100
all at the same time.

00:11:38.180 --> 00:11:40.150
Robert, what about you?

00:11:40.150 --> 00:11:41.300
How's the process been?

00:11:43.597 --> 00:11:45.460
- The process has been...

00:11:47.470 --> 00:11:49.450
It's been a lot, it's been...

00:11:51.400 --> 00:11:56.400
I think to kind of be
present in this moment

00:11:56.621 --> 00:12:01.621
and hear what Sam, Pisay
and Sonnie are saying,

00:12:02.702 --> 00:12:06.430
it is definitely highlighting
some of the emotions

00:12:08.730 --> 00:12:10.970
and some of the struggles

00:12:10.970 --> 00:12:13.120
that have stuck out the most, for sure.

00:12:13.120 --> 00:12:18.063
And living within this
collective time of uncertainty,

00:12:21.070 --> 00:12:22.780
definitely has been a lot of that.

00:12:22.780 --> 00:12:26.260
And I think a lot of the days,

00:12:26.260 --> 00:12:31.260
I've tried to be as fully
present and committed and kind of

00:12:36.040 --> 00:12:37.943
try to put aside some of that.

00:12:40.990 --> 00:12:45.183
Wanting things to be headed somewhere,

00:12:47.330 --> 00:12:49.990
and there's been a weird
relationship with time.

00:12:49.990 --> 00:12:53.430
I think my concept of time
has always been very strange,

00:12:53.430 --> 00:12:56.800
like early on in rehearsals,

00:12:56.800 --> 00:12:58.950
I would have this
feeling every day of like

00:12:59.920 --> 00:13:03.230
we come here every day
and every day is new,

00:13:03.230 --> 00:13:06.930
but then we're doing the
same things, and it's like,

00:13:06.930 --> 00:13:09.060
this is how we choose to live our lives.

00:13:09.060 --> 00:13:10.490
Like we come every day,

00:13:10.490 --> 00:13:14.380
we commit and we put so much
hard work into doing this

00:13:14.380 --> 00:13:15.520
with so many hours every day.

00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:17.210
And I'm like, this is really beautiful,

00:13:17.210 --> 00:13:18.950
but it's also such a...

00:13:18.950 --> 00:13:21.790
I think it could be kind
of overlooked of like

00:13:22.700 --> 00:13:23.860
you do this every day.

00:13:23.860 --> 00:13:26.810
Like you commit your time on this planet

00:13:26.810 --> 00:13:27.740
to doing this every day.

00:13:27.740 --> 00:13:29.940
And I'm like, yeah,
like this is what we do.

00:13:29.940 --> 00:13:32.120
And then with all of the uncertainty,

00:13:32.120 --> 00:13:34.080
it just adds kind of like a weight to it,

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:35.493
an extra layer weight to it.

00:13:38.340 --> 00:13:41.402
But overall it has been
amazing in so many ways

00:13:41.402 --> 00:13:46.402
that I think are so many
things have been challenging,

00:13:46.420 --> 00:13:47.960
challenging and painful,

00:13:47.960 --> 00:13:52.960
and like pulling open of yourself,

00:13:57.320 --> 00:14:01.860
but overall has been amazing
and I think it's such a...

00:14:03.330 --> 00:14:07.022
Grateful to have this
experience so far, still going.

00:14:07.022 --> 00:14:09.727
- Still going, still going.

00:14:09.727 --> 00:14:10.930
Well, I appreciate that
level of candor too

00:14:10.930 --> 00:14:11.763
as you all have been sharing,

00:14:11.763 --> 00:14:13.640
just the process you all
have been going through

00:14:13.640 --> 00:14:15.590
cause again, that's not
something I think people

00:14:15.590 --> 00:14:18.450
wouldn't necessarily see at the front end

00:14:18.450 --> 00:14:21.100
walking into the play,
but at the same time

00:14:21.100 --> 00:14:22.470
as you all have been sharing,

00:14:22.470 --> 00:14:24.027
some thoughts are coming to mind as well,

00:14:24.027 --> 00:14:25.300
one because again,

00:14:25.300 --> 00:14:26.970
there were so many aspects of the play

00:14:26.970 --> 00:14:28.770
that you all pulled in together.

00:14:28.770 --> 00:14:32.260
I mean, aspects of
memory, aspects of trauma,

00:14:32.260 --> 00:14:36.080
racism, race relations, assimilation,

00:14:36.080 --> 00:14:38.480
refugee status, family dynamics.

00:14:38.480 --> 00:14:41.130
And obviously I'm biased
in watching it play too,

00:14:41.130 --> 00:14:42.930
because I felt like all
the topics in the play were

00:14:42.930 --> 00:14:45.690
all the things that were
about on this podcast as well

00:14:45.690 --> 00:14:48.590
so it was really nice to
see that brought to life

00:14:48.590 --> 00:14:49.580
in a sense,

00:14:49.580 --> 00:14:50.900
but then even watching the preview

00:14:50.900 --> 00:14:53.123
and this may have just been
me too, but I felt like

00:14:53.123 --> 00:14:56.040
a lot of the emotions that
were in the play really kind of

00:14:56.040 --> 00:14:59.500
left off the stage in a sense
and felt very real to me

00:14:59.500 --> 00:15:00.333
in that moment.

00:15:00.333 --> 00:15:02.270
And so I'm also just curious as you all,

00:15:02.270 --> 00:15:03.500
cause you've touched on this already,

00:15:03.500 --> 00:15:05.560
just your own life experiences

00:15:05.560 --> 00:15:08.460
and how this has been
one of the most powerful

00:15:08.460 --> 00:15:09.440
roles that you've been in

00:15:09.440 --> 00:15:13.230
and how has your life
experience within this moment

00:15:13.230 --> 00:15:15.100
and just in general impacted

00:15:15.100 --> 00:15:17.830
how you've walked into the art in a sense.

00:15:17.830 --> 00:15:20.210
And I'd actually just be curious to see

00:15:20.210 --> 00:15:21.650
those emotions that I seemed the sense

00:15:21.650 --> 00:15:22.820
that seemed so genuine to me,

00:15:22.820 --> 00:15:25.950
was that completely you all
being just that excellent

00:15:25.950 --> 00:15:27.990
in your craft or were
there aspects of yourselves

00:15:27.990 --> 00:15:30.640
that also came into that as well?

00:15:30.640 --> 00:15:33.410
So again, I'm asking lots
of questions at once,

00:15:33.410 --> 00:15:35.810
but I'm just curious if you
all would want to comment

00:15:35.810 --> 00:15:39.350
on any of those comments or aspects.

00:15:39.350 --> 00:15:41.160
Robert, I might start with you
to kind of reverse the order

00:15:41.160 --> 00:15:43.353
this time around, not
to put you on the spot.

00:15:46.610 --> 00:15:48.980
- Just like how our life
has been our journey

00:15:48.980 --> 00:15:49.850
leading up to this point,

00:15:49.850 --> 00:15:52.613
like how it's kind of
like blended in together?

00:15:55.160 --> 00:15:56.633
Just processing it myself.

00:16:01.660 --> 00:16:03.773
I think it had to have happened like this.

00:16:08.040 --> 00:16:11.900
At times, I do kind of think about

00:16:12.770 --> 00:16:16.160
fate in a way and some things are

00:16:16.160 --> 00:16:18.420
too much to be just coincidences.

00:16:18.420 --> 00:16:23.420
And I think there's so many
things I don't understand,

00:16:24.100 --> 00:16:28.683
but sometimes things
just kind of make sense

00:16:31.680 --> 00:16:32.920
in some weird existential way

00:16:32.920 --> 00:16:34.973
and I think that in terms of my life,

00:16:36.130 --> 00:16:38.610
I feel like there's so
many stages in my life

00:16:38.610 --> 00:16:40.780
where I just got completely lucky,

00:16:40.780 --> 00:16:43.935
just without really being
able to understand it,

00:16:43.935 --> 00:16:46.680
just feeling like I just got lucky somehow

00:16:46.680 --> 00:16:50.890
and starting this process being here

00:16:50.890 --> 00:16:52.270
and having these conversations

00:16:52.270 --> 00:16:57.020
with all these great people, I'm like oh,

00:16:57.020 --> 00:16:59.210
it seems like all that luck

00:16:59.210 --> 00:17:04.210
kind of like contributes to
a purpose where I'm like, oh,

00:17:05.670 --> 00:17:07.247
well then it kind of like
makes you look at things

00:17:07.247 --> 00:17:09.547
and the past a little
differently of like, oh,

00:17:11.460 --> 00:17:12.950
a lot of those things needed to happen

00:17:12.950 --> 00:17:14.500
for this to be possible.

00:17:14.500 --> 00:17:17.340
I think for me to allow myself to do this,

00:17:17.340 --> 00:17:21.750
to see things in this way,
to try to work in this way,

00:17:21.750 --> 00:17:26.090
to be giving and accepting
of a lot of these processes,

00:17:26.090 --> 00:17:27.973
which are difficult.

00:17:30.800 --> 00:17:35.740
And I think something quickly is that like

00:17:35.740 --> 00:17:37.490
I think I lived a certain way where

00:17:40.450 --> 00:17:43.350
somehow I continue to stay
really curious about things

00:17:43.350 --> 00:17:45.400
and right now, like even in this moment,

00:17:45.400 --> 00:17:50.340
throughout this rehearsal
play process is like...

00:17:50.340 --> 00:17:54.207
I feel like as a man
walking through the world

00:17:58.710 --> 00:18:02.980
slowly get into a place of like putting

00:18:02.980 --> 00:18:07.030
a lot of those things down, which is hard,

00:18:07.030 --> 00:18:09.310
but I feel like the more I put down

00:18:10.790 --> 00:18:14.740
in terms of what it means to be a man

00:18:14.740 --> 00:18:17.080
or what I've conceptualized in my mind

00:18:17.080 --> 00:18:19.400
from a kid up until now is that like,

00:18:19.400 --> 00:18:21.080
the more I let go, the more I put down,

00:18:21.080 --> 00:18:24.350
the more I kind of like break down

00:18:24.350 --> 00:18:26.390
and let kind of dissolve,

00:18:26.390 --> 00:18:30.900
like the more I feel like
I can really be of service

00:18:30.900 --> 00:18:32.600
and really be a part of something.

00:18:34.580 --> 00:18:38.320
And I think I'm being vague,

00:18:38.320 --> 00:18:43.320
but being around all of the great women

00:18:43.540 --> 00:18:45.890
that I've had to a chance to talk to

00:18:45.890 --> 00:18:49.610
and kind of trust and
just open up to has been

00:18:50.940 --> 00:18:55.387
a gift in a very kind
of like personal way.

00:18:58.391 --> 00:19:00.950
And I think living my life has been...

00:19:02.045 --> 00:19:07.045
It feels almost a 180,
like I grew up at a time

00:19:07.100 --> 00:19:10.660
where in my neighborhood,
the people I grew up with,

00:19:10.660 --> 00:19:12.770
the ideology of people around me

00:19:12.770 --> 00:19:17.770
was just so toxic, and
then just even living,

00:19:18.780 --> 00:19:21.610
growing up in a Cambodia American family,

00:19:21.610 --> 00:19:25.170
it's like there's so much
silence and there's so much

00:19:28.200 --> 00:19:32.350
pain and violence and just
chaos like all the time.

00:19:32.350 --> 00:19:34.380
And then it's like I feel lucky

00:19:34.380 --> 00:19:39.380
that I was able to kind of like
maintain my myself somehow.

00:19:39.910 --> 00:19:42.000
And this process is a continuation of that

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:44.113
I feel like if that makes sense.

00:19:47.324 --> 00:19:48.904
Thank you.

00:19:48.904 --> 00:19:49.860
- Of course and thanks for sharing too.

00:19:49.860 --> 00:19:53.650
I mean, just that level
of openness as well,

00:19:53.650 --> 00:19:54.870
and it may feel vague,

00:19:54.870 --> 00:19:56.450
but also not vague in a lot of ways, too.

00:19:56.450 --> 00:19:57.770
I think just understanding

00:19:57.770 --> 00:20:00.150
what you're getting at and just

00:20:00.150 --> 00:20:02.570
the great women you've
been able to be around.

00:20:02.570 --> 00:20:03.907
It sounds like in this production as well,

00:20:03.907 --> 00:20:06.090
and just the learning process has been

00:20:06.090 --> 00:20:08.910
so thanks for sharing that,
definitely appreciate it.

00:20:10.861 --> 00:20:12.270
Sonnie, what about what
about you in terms of...

00:20:12.270 --> 00:20:14.940
I mean, you mentioned this
was one of the roles that you,

00:20:14.940 --> 00:20:17.360
or the role that you've enjoyed the most

00:20:17.360 --> 00:20:20.330
and how much of that has just
been from the character itself

00:20:20.330 --> 00:20:21.890
and how much of that is from other...

00:20:21.890 --> 00:20:24.990
What about this role really
brought you to that place

00:20:24.990 --> 00:20:26.630
to say that?

00:20:26.630 --> 00:20:28.420
- Well, she goes on a journey,

00:20:28.420 --> 00:20:33.420
like a very profound,
serious journey of character

00:20:33.460 --> 00:20:37.300
from a place of denial kind of,

00:20:37.300 --> 00:20:42.300
of wanting to not share
the trauma in her life

00:20:43.620 --> 00:20:44.920
to protect her children.

00:20:44.920 --> 00:20:47.220
From that knowledge, I feel like at least

00:20:47.220 --> 00:20:50.500
that's how I'm playing
her, because I feel like

00:20:50.500 --> 00:20:53.230
you experience horrific things.

00:20:53.230 --> 00:20:55.730
Everyone does, and there
are levels of trauma

00:20:55.730 --> 00:20:57.550
that people go through

00:20:57.550 --> 00:21:02.300
that can seem nothing to some one person,

00:21:02.300 --> 00:21:05.520
but to that person, it is just as real

00:21:05.520 --> 00:21:09.910
and me wanting to protect
the kids from that,

00:21:09.910 --> 00:21:12.610
from knowing the horrors that I've seen,

00:21:12.610 --> 00:21:14.430
cause I want them to live a normal life

00:21:14.430 --> 00:21:16.363
that is without that.

00:21:18.180 --> 00:21:21.330
But then it kind of starts to
come home for the character

00:21:21.330 --> 00:21:23.040
where in a very real way,

00:21:23.040 --> 00:21:26.810
like she's losing her grip on her family,

00:21:26.810 --> 00:21:28.290
and they're going away

00:21:28.290 --> 00:21:31.010
and not wanting to spend time with her

00:21:31.010 --> 00:21:33.620
and I feel like in a desperate act,

00:21:33.620 --> 00:21:35.470
she's like okay, I will try this,

00:21:35.470 --> 00:21:37.653
give you what you are asking of me,

00:21:38.870 --> 00:21:40.710
but it ends up freeing her too

00:21:40.710 --> 00:21:45.710
in a way where she can
release toxicity inside of her

00:21:46.790 --> 00:21:50.520
and be able to do things that
she thought were dead to her

00:21:50.520 --> 00:21:51.493
all of these years.

00:21:52.350 --> 00:21:54.960
I feel like that's a very real journey

00:21:54.960 --> 00:21:56.530
that a lot of people go through.

00:21:56.530 --> 00:21:59.713
Personally how I bring that to life,

00:22:02.410 --> 00:22:06.690
we all actors have their
little secret bags and

00:22:07.720 --> 00:22:10.500
you don't talk too much about them

00:22:10.500 --> 00:22:12.710
because they are your secret things

00:22:12.710 --> 00:22:16.490
and not everyone needs
to know where you mine

00:22:16.490 --> 00:22:18.093
some of that gold from.

00:22:21.703 --> 00:22:23.530
But in a very real way on stage,

00:22:23.530 --> 00:22:27.940
I try to make it as like I
have two children on stage

00:22:27.940 --> 00:22:31.833
and I love them deeply and I
would die for them if I had to.

00:22:32.799 --> 00:22:35.040
And that's a very real feeling,

00:22:35.040 --> 00:22:36.430
especially if you have a child,

00:22:36.430 --> 00:22:38.843
you understand that in your soul.

00:22:39.720 --> 00:22:41.593
If something were to happen,

00:22:42.900 --> 00:22:47.530
you would easily gratefully
die for those children.

00:22:47.530 --> 00:22:49.380
It's just something that's universal.

00:22:51.210 --> 00:22:56.070
And so I can bring that
much of myself as a mother

00:22:56.930 --> 00:22:57.763
to that role.

00:22:57.763 --> 00:22:59.483
And then the rest of the trauma,

00:23:02.110 --> 00:23:04.800
it's not that difficult
these days to be able to draw

00:23:04.800 --> 00:23:09.800
on the horrors of the
war, and use some of that.

00:23:10.990 --> 00:23:12.540
But I usually...

00:23:14.110 --> 00:23:17.950
As things that I on to
prepare to go to a dark place

00:23:17.950 --> 00:23:20.860
for two hours every day,
for six days a week,

00:23:20.860 --> 00:23:23.560
or sometimes four hours,
depending on which day it is,

00:23:24.820 --> 00:23:27.620
you have to go into a very
dark place in your head,

00:23:27.620 --> 00:23:32.620
and I've been able to do it fairly quickly

00:23:33.130 --> 00:23:35.470
where I don't have to
live there all day long

00:23:35.470 --> 00:23:38.240
because that can be a very
difficult person to live with

00:23:38.240 --> 00:23:39.113
for my family.

00:23:40.760 --> 00:23:44.100
So yeah, it's honing the craft,

00:23:44.100 --> 00:23:49.100
knowing how to access the
good and the bad in yourself

00:23:50.220 --> 00:23:53.070
to try to bring it so
people can relate to it

00:23:53.070 --> 00:23:54.823
in a very meaningful way.

00:23:57.330 --> 00:24:00.033
But the core of it, for me,

00:24:00.910 --> 00:24:03.163
all of this performance is all about love.

00:24:06.450 --> 00:24:10.040
- And that comes through
so strongly as well.

00:24:10.040 --> 00:24:12.260
So I think you were
definitely effective in that

00:24:12.260 --> 00:24:15.100
and just the way you were
able to bring all those parts

00:24:15.100 --> 00:24:16.630
of yourself into the character

00:24:16.630 --> 00:24:19.748
cause that love definitely
came across as well

00:24:19.748 --> 00:24:22.169
and just the journey that you were on

00:24:22.169 --> 00:24:23.753
as the mother as well.

00:24:25.110 --> 00:24:26.763
Thanks so much for sharing.

00:24:28.009 --> 00:24:29.620
Pisay, come back to you as well,

00:24:29.620 --> 00:24:32.390
cause again my comments about the emotion,

00:24:32.390 --> 00:24:35.800
like I felt it throughout
and with all of the members

00:24:35.800 --> 00:24:38.410
of the cast, but especially
with your character

00:24:38.410 --> 00:24:40.282
in particular, like a
lot of that just felt

00:24:40.282 --> 00:24:44.330
so honest and real, authentic
and raw all the same time.

00:24:44.330 --> 00:24:46.470
So just curious how you were able

00:24:46.470 --> 00:24:49.583
to hone the craft even as
Sonnie was talking about

00:24:49.583 --> 00:24:52.600
so effectively in that space
and how much of that again

00:24:52.600 --> 00:24:54.760
was from a personal place or even

00:24:54.760 --> 00:24:56.730
just being that much in the craft

00:24:56.730 --> 00:24:59.263
or any of those pieces in between.

00:25:00.500 --> 00:25:03.703
- Thank you for that, I
appreciate you saying that.

00:25:04.820 --> 00:25:06.580
When you first asked the question,

00:25:06.580 --> 00:25:09.880
my stomach immediately started to churn.

00:25:09.880 --> 00:25:12.830
I was like, oh my God, thank
God he's starting with Robert.

00:25:14.610 --> 00:25:17.100
And then I was like oh, okay.

00:25:17.100 --> 00:25:19.103
What does that mean?

00:25:21.370 --> 00:25:22.383
When I first auditioned for it,

00:25:22.383 --> 00:25:27.383
there's a scene and I was sort
of practicing with my partner

00:25:29.656 --> 00:25:33.310
and we do a lot of that
especially nowadays

00:25:33.310 --> 00:25:35.968
when everything is sort of done over Zoom

00:25:35.968 --> 00:25:38.690
and he turned off the camera

00:25:38.690 --> 00:25:40.513
and he was really concerned for me,

00:25:41.360 --> 00:25:45.740
really concerned about the
words that I was saying and how

00:25:46.830 --> 00:25:50.270
it felt so connected to
what my life has been

00:25:50.270 --> 00:25:52.220
and what he knows of me.

00:25:52.220 --> 00:25:54.340
And so my challenge has been,

00:25:54.340 --> 00:25:58.060
because Pia is a different
human being for me

00:25:58.060 --> 00:26:02.350
as much as I connect with her
on a lot of different levels,

00:26:02.350 --> 00:26:04.720
my challenge was sort of really trying

00:26:04.720 --> 00:26:06.370
to find that separation

00:26:07.752 --> 00:26:12.030
and make sure that I
honor her as a character.

00:26:12.030 --> 00:26:13.480
But of course there were things

00:26:13.480 --> 00:26:18.020
that came in that couldn't be helped.

00:26:18.020 --> 00:26:20.223
And then when those moments come,

00:26:22.120 --> 00:26:23.310
the thing with live theater is

00:26:23.310 --> 00:26:27.370
you gotta go with it, which
we've been doing with like

00:26:27.370 --> 00:26:32.063
the cell phones going off
and the pipes going off.

00:26:34.040 --> 00:26:37.060
And I think I find for me,

00:26:37.060 --> 00:26:40.070
I find character through other people,

00:26:40.070 --> 00:26:45.010
through real people and or
through movies that I've watched.

00:26:45.010 --> 00:26:48.730
And in this case I was sharing with Sam

00:26:48.730 --> 00:26:53.373
I have a relative who
reminds me so much of Pia.

00:26:55.419 --> 00:26:58.810
She is talking about growing up and having

00:26:58.810 --> 00:27:02.540
to run to her neighbor's house because

00:27:02.540 --> 00:27:05.360
her parents were so scary.

00:27:05.360 --> 00:27:08.700
But when she talks about,
it's just sort of like

00:27:08.700 --> 00:27:12.000
as if she's sharing her
favorite ice cream shop

00:27:12.000 --> 00:27:13.610
from when she was a kid,

00:27:13.610 --> 00:27:17.310
it's not like it was super
traumatic for her and oh my God,

00:27:17.310 --> 00:27:18.310
like save me.

00:27:18.310 --> 00:27:21.353
It was just more like, oh
yeah, this is what happened.

00:27:22.370 --> 00:27:25.310
So I drew a lot from that experience.

00:27:25.310 --> 00:27:30.310
I have a friend who is an audiologist,

00:27:30.310 --> 00:27:33.424
totally different from
being a neuroscientist,

00:27:33.424 --> 00:27:35.580
but went through all the schooling.

00:27:35.580 --> 00:27:38.960
And at one intersection of her life,

00:27:38.960 --> 00:27:41.220
she did have to make a sacrifice

00:27:41.220 --> 00:27:45.540
and not go to a particular school

00:27:45.540 --> 00:27:48.493
in order to help her family.

00:27:49.610 --> 00:27:53.980
And so I've been lucky enough
to draw from the community

00:27:55.460 --> 00:28:00.460
that I have, and I was
telling Robert this too

00:28:00.810 --> 00:28:01.873
when we first met, I was like,

00:28:01.873 --> 00:28:03.950
I am not that kind of Cambodian

00:28:03.950 --> 00:28:07.210
because I didn't grow up in a place

00:28:07.210 --> 00:28:08.360
like little Massachusetts,

00:28:08.360 --> 00:28:10.170
I didn't grow up in
Long Beach, California,

00:28:10.170 --> 00:28:12.430
where there really is a very...

00:28:12.430 --> 00:28:16.000
There's a bigger Cambodian
community and everyone's sort of

00:28:16.000 --> 00:28:19.530
interacting and speaking with
one another and hanging out.

00:28:19.530 --> 00:28:24.080
I grew up in a very,
mostly white neighborhood.

00:28:24.080 --> 00:28:25.140
And so in some ways,

00:28:25.140 --> 00:28:29.233
I was sort of ostracized
from my own community,

00:28:30.190 --> 00:28:35.190
but no one, like obviously
no matter where I go,

00:28:36.140 --> 00:28:39.270
if I meet another Khmer
person, so welcoming,

00:28:39.270 --> 00:28:43.310
because our community
has been so separated.

00:28:43.310 --> 00:28:47.460
So I feel really blessed to
be able to talk to my cousin

00:28:47.460 --> 00:28:48.960
who I haven't spoken to in years,

00:28:48.960 --> 00:28:52.450
or to talk to my girlfriend
also haven't spoken to in years

00:28:52.450 --> 00:28:54.843
to feel that community
and that connection.

00:28:56.540 --> 00:29:01.540
And then also the words on
the page are everything.

00:29:03.490 --> 00:29:07.040
I don't have to do a ton
of work because everything

00:29:07.040 --> 00:29:08.780
that Pia is saying and everything

00:29:08.780 --> 00:29:10.353
the other character's saying,

00:29:11.890 --> 00:29:14.433
it's just like how can you not feel.

00:29:15.270 --> 00:29:17.690
When something's true,
when something's honest,

00:29:17.690 --> 00:29:20.800
it's very, very hard to turn away from it.

00:29:20.800 --> 00:29:25.453
And that also applies to Robert and Sonnie

00:29:25.453 --> 00:29:30.453
and Emma and Kiran, who
came with such honesty.

00:29:30.870 --> 00:29:34.920
It's how do you turn away
from your scene partner

00:29:34.920 --> 00:29:39.310
when they're giving
you so much to play on.

00:29:39.310 --> 00:29:41.520
I was really worried
about the very ending,

00:29:41.520 --> 00:29:44.160
like if I could get emotional every night

00:29:44.160 --> 00:29:45.860
and I'm like thank God for Sonnie,

00:29:47.600 --> 00:29:49.680
because just looking at her

00:29:50.700 --> 00:29:53.360
and seeing what she's going
through and telling her story,

00:29:53.360 --> 00:29:54.790
and then listening to those words,

00:29:54.790 --> 00:29:57.300
a combination of those things,

00:29:57.300 --> 00:30:02.300
how can you not be so
moved by what this woman

00:30:03.130 --> 00:30:06.820
has gone through and the bravery

00:30:06.820 --> 00:30:10.910
of having lived through that
and raising two children

00:30:10.910 --> 00:30:14.310
and then sharing it with her daughter.

00:30:14.310 --> 00:30:17.783
So it was a combination of both things.

00:30:18.910 --> 00:30:19.743
- Yeah, really well said.

00:30:19.743 --> 00:30:22.430
And that authenticity definitely came out

00:30:22.430 --> 00:30:24.380
throughout the production too.

00:30:24.380 --> 00:30:26.930
I mean, even as I'm listening
to you all talk about it

00:30:26.930 --> 00:30:29.910
and thinking back to the production and

00:30:29.910 --> 00:30:33.780
having glimpses and flashbacks
isn't the best word to use,

00:30:33.780 --> 00:30:36.200
but thinking about how you
all looked at each other

00:30:36.200 --> 00:30:39.060
in certain moments, everything
you just said really...

00:30:39.060 --> 00:30:39.893
I mean, I can think back

00:30:39.893 --> 00:30:41.480
and see that and sense that,

00:30:41.480 --> 00:30:43.150
I think that's part of what was giving me

00:30:43.150 --> 00:30:46.090
that feeling as well as you all entered

00:30:46.090 --> 00:30:48.560
into those spaces in a lot of ways.

00:30:48.560 --> 00:30:49.520
So really appreciate, again,

00:30:49.520 --> 00:30:52.960
the way that you all have gone
there in a brave way as well

00:30:52.960 --> 00:30:54.110
cause even as Sonnie mentioned,

00:30:54.110 --> 00:30:56.690
that's not a place that
you can stay all day long

00:30:57.730 --> 00:31:00.140
for any number of reasons,

00:31:00.140 --> 00:31:02.950
but just the way that you
all are able to even it out

00:31:02.950 --> 00:31:04.430
and then as you all are talking,

00:31:04.430 --> 00:31:07.620
I get a sense of community
amongst the cast members

00:31:07.620 --> 00:31:09.450
and amongst the production
crew and everybody as well,

00:31:09.450 --> 00:31:13.150
which I think also builds
into what you all are doing.

00:31:13.150 --> 00:31:14.740
So again, just really grateful for the way

00:31:14.740 --> 00:31:16.674
that you all have captured that

00:31:16.674 --> 00:31:18.540
and are sharing that with audiences.

00:31:18.540 --> 00:31:20.850
Sam, I did want to loop
back to you as well.

00:31:20.850 --> 00:31:22.710
Again, as a person who has written this,

00:31:22.710 --> 00:31:25.260
I'm gonna mention a quote
that I saw on your website

00:31:25.260 --> 00:31:27.450
cause I really loved it the
way that you talked about

00:31:27.450 --> 00:31:31.720
your work, exploring interactions
and collisions of race,

00:31:31.720 --> 00:31:36.040
gender, science, and technology,
and centering characters

00:31:36.040 --> 00:31:39.140
occupying some kind of marginal space.

00:31:39.140 --> 00:31:41.380
You also mentioned that you
hope to contribute to efforts

00:31:41.380 --> 00:31:44.640
to illuminate and elevate
marginalized voices.

00:31:44.640 --> 00:31:47.350
I think that's just so powerful.

00:31:47.350 --> 00:31:50.051
It's definitely the case
for me when I, again,

00:31:50.051 --> 00:31:53.100
when I watch the production and
have seen that in your work.

00:31:53.100 --> 00:31:55.860
But I also just wanted to
talk a little bit about...

00:31:55.860 --> 00:31:57.590
Cause this authenticity
has really come out

00:31:57.590 --> 00:31:59.830
in this conversation,
something I sense as well.

00:31:59.830 --> 00:32:02.000
How have you been able to really craft

00:32:02.000 --> 00:32:04.830
that sense of authenticity in your work

00:32:04.830 --> 00:32:06.410
even as you're are elevating

00:32:06.410 --> 00:32:08.110
some of these marginalized voices?

00:32:09.420 --> 00:32:13.910
- Well, thanks for pointing
to that description.

00:32:13.910 --> 00:32:16.670
I just wanna say that I write that

00:32:16.670 --> 00:32:19.910
as a way to understand
what I do as an impulse.

00:32:19.910 --> 00:32:21.260
I think that a lot of us do that,

00:32:21.260 --> 00:32:24.820
I feel like a lot of us who are members,

00:32:24.820 --> 00:32:28.570
who are in communities that are
marginalized and decentered,

00:32:28.570 --> 00:32:31.730
not centered, naturally have that impulse.

00:32:31.730 --> 00:32:34.330
I mean, it's just speaking
from our own truth

00:32:34.330 --> 00:32:36.560
and telling the stories that we know,

00:32:36.560 --> 00:32:39.470
which have not been
centered and haven't been...

00:32:41.520 --> 00:32:42.810
And aren't widely recognized.

00:32:42.810 --> 00:32:46.030
So I just guess I just
wanted to say too that

00:32:46.030 --> 00:32:49.590
when I write that and it is trying

00:32:49.590 --> 00:32:53.920
to describe what I think a lot
of us do naturally as artists

00:32:53.920 --> 00:32:57.503
who are making theater,
like all of us on this call.

00:32:58.540 --> 00:33:02.877
So I feel it's just
part of what the stories

00:33:04.220 --> 00:33:05.540
that I'm drawn to and the stories

00:33:05.540 --> 00:33:10.530
that are in me that I wanna give voice to,

00:33:10.530 --> 00:33:11.650
or that I naturally give.

00:33:11.650 --> 00:33:14.860
Those are the ones that just emerge

00:33:14.860 --> 00:33:18.450
because they're part of the people

00:33:18.450 --> 00:33:20.557
who have been part of my lives and my life

00:33:20.557 --> 00:33:25.020
and the people who I
interact with and the stories

00:33:25.020 --> 00:33:29.580
I identify with and in
terms of how I wouldn't...

00:33:29.580 --> 00:33:33.000
I don't know how, I just know that

00:33:33.000 --> 00:33:34.360
whenever I'm writing something

00:33:34.360 --> 00:33:36.080
and this is true for this play,

00:33:36.080 --> 00:33:39.270
it's always about how am I connecting?

00:33:39.270 --> 00:33:43.670
Like I'm always connecting
with the characters

00:33:43.670 --> 00:33:44.780
and the journey that they're on

00:33:44.780 --> 00:33:48.940
and I wouldn't write it

00:33:48.940 --> 00:33:52.400
if I wasn't identifying
strongly with them.

00:33:52.400 --> 00:33:54.050
And I don't know how that will...

00:33:55.450 --> 00:33:57.670
If that will resonate or not,

00:33:57.670 --> 00:34:02.670
but there's a kind of gravity to the...

00:34:03.610 --> 00:34:08.500
I feel that I'm drawn to certain material

00:34:10.170 --> 00:34:11.730
and characters and stories.

00:34:11.730 --> 00:34:15.400
And then maybe that's when I'm writing,

00:34:15.400 --> 00:34:20.300
I'm writing from that
perspective and with this play,

00:34:20.300 --> 00:34:23.600
I feel like I really
connected with the mother,

00:34:23.600 --> 00:34:25.060
like with the parent and child

00:34:25.060 --> 00:34:27.390
and the silences that are in the family.

00:34:27.390 --> 00:34:29.130
I think that that's
something that a lot of us

00:34:29.130 --> 00:34:32.880
have grown up with, and
I'm not Cambodian American.

00:34:32.880 --> 00:34:35.883
My dad's Chinese, my mom
is Pennsylvania Dutch.

00:34:40.572 --> 00:34:42.540
But there was something about the quality

00:34:42.540 --> 00:34:46.140
of the silences and the
inability to connect

00:34:46.140 --> 00:34:47.980
and the longing to connect

00:34:47.980 --> 00:34:51.330
and all of the different
kinds of secrets and traumas

00:34:51.330 --> 00:34:52.710
that are buried in families

00:34:52.710 --> 00:34:54.710
and I think especially in...

00:34:54.710 --> 00:34:55.543
Well, not especially,

00:34:55.543 --> 00:34:57.740
but I know in a lot of immigrant families

00:34:57.740 --> 00:35:02.740
and I just immediately felt this

00:35:05.580 --> 00:35:08.450
belonging to understand your history,

00:35:08.450 --> 00:35:10.170
your own history differently and feel

00:35:10.170 --> 00:35:12.218
that maybe there are other possibilities

00:35:12.218 --> 00:35:14.180
for what has already been written for you

00:35:14.180 --> 00:35:17.270
or something that has
already been imposed on you,

00:35:17.270 --> 00:35:21.470
but that if you could have some ability

00:35:21.470 --> 00:35:24.270
to connect differently
with members of your family

00:35:24.270 --> 00:35:29.270
and understand your
yourself in a different way

00:35:29.610 --> 00:35:32.250
and somehow have...

00:35:33.150 --> 00:35:37.080
I think there's a longing to do that,

00:35:37.080 --> 00:35:39.430
that I had the longing
to do that with family

00:35:39.430 --> 00:35:44.430
to be able to transform
relationships somehow.

00:35:44.740 --> 00:35:46.090
- Understandable, but I mean,

00:35:46.090 --> 00:35:48.810
that was so well said in a
lot of ways too cause I think,

00:35:48.810 --> 00:35:52.170
again what you just brought
out in the character

00:35:52.170 --> 00:35:56.830
is just that I dunno if
to call it discomfort,

00:35:56.830 --> 00:35:58.760
but just that uncomfortable silence.

00:35:58.760 --> 00:36:02.820
And I felt like you all as cast
members were just excellent

00:36:02.820 --> 00:36:04.970
and pulling out what Sam
had written into that too

00:36:04.970 --> 00:36:08.470
cause I find myself following
you all in that moment

00:36:08.470 --> 00:36:10.240
and reacting with you and say, oh,

00:36:10.240 --> 00:36:11.430
I wish this could come out here.

00:36:11.430 --> 00:36:12.800
I wish this come out so it just, again,

00:36:12.800 --> 00:36:14.680
felt so authentic and real,

00:36:14.680 --> 00:36:15.750
I think in a lot of ways that people

00:36:15.750 --> 00:36:18.350
would connect with that as well.

00:36:18.350 --> 00:36:20.210
And then, I mean, to be completely honest,

00:36:20.210 --> 00:36:22.570
also watching it as a neuroscientist

00:36:22.570 --> 00:36:25.840
and that can be good and bad
because I'm thinking about,

00:36:25.840 --> 00:36:27.750
well what's happening in
the brain at this moment.

00:36:27.750 --> 00:36:29.730
Oh, I wish this learned behavior

00:36:29.730 --> 00:36:30.950
could change here and this...

00:36:30.950 --> 00:36:33.990
So, I mean, in a sense you all
pulled me along for the ride

00:36:33.990 --> 00:36:36.810
in an authentic way that
was having me walk with you,

00:36:36.810 --> 00:36:38.700
but also yearning for
things to be different,

00:36:38.700 --> 00:36:40.150
thinking about what's
happening in the brain.

00:36:40.150 --> 00:36:42.420
So on lots of different levels, again,

00:36:42.420 --> 00:36:44.730
I think it was really
just important to have

00:36:44.730 --> 00:36:48.920
those stories told in such
authentic and powerful ways,

00:36:48.920 --> 00:36:51.485
but on that level of
authenticity as well and again,

00:36:51.485 --> 00:36:54.740
this is something that's
not maybe as generalizable,

00:36:54.740 --> 00:36:56.450
but again, just watching
as a neuroscientist,

00:36:56.450 --> 00:36:58.920
I think a lot of scientists
from different fields

00:36:58.920 --> 00:37:01.430
who might watch the play
will also be encouraged

00:37:01.430 --> 00:37:04.170
to see how well you all integrated

00:37:04.170 --> 00:37:06.120
some of those scientific concepts, again,

00:37:06.120 --> 00:37:09.250
in a thoughtful and authentic way

00:37:09.250 --> 00:37:10.690
that had real life application,

00:37:10.690 --> 00:37:13.510
cause there's always that tension as well.

00:37:13.510 --> 00:37:14.710
When Sam and I were talking,

00:37:14.710 --> 00:37:16.230
one of the things we talked about as well

00:37:16.230 --> 00:37:18.850
was just this tension
that sometimes comes up

00:37:18.850 --> 00:37:22.070
within scientific communities
of promising new therapies

00:37:22.070 --> 00:37:23.380
and new changes,

00:37:23.380 --> 00:37:26.010
but then having that balance
of how realistic is this.

00:37:26.010 --> 00:37:28.350
Is this something that we
can see in one to two years,

00:37:28.350 --> 00:37:31.700
is this something five,
10, 20 years down the road?

00:37:31.700 --> 00:37:33.190
And so again, from a
point of authenticity,

00:37:33.190 --> 00:37:35.310
it was really nice for
me to see that interplay

00:37:35.310 --> 00:37:39.030
and that tension also
play out in the production

00:37:39.030 --> 00:37:42.280
as you all were talking
about memories and traumas

00:37:42.280 --> 00:37:45.078
and whether those things
could be rewritten

00:37:45.078 --> 00:37:48.680
and removed even, but then
also this tension of, well,

00:37:48.680 --> 00:37:50.840
is this something that we
can only do in rats and mice

00:37:50.840 --> 00:37:54.200
or does this actually have
applicability to humans as well?

00:37:54.200 --> 00:37:56.990
So without going into the
weeds as a neuroscientist,

00:37:56.990 --> 00:37:59.710
cause that that's a tendency
that could definitely

00:37:59.710 --> 00:38:01.120
lean for us but I did wanted to mention

00:38:01.120 --> 00:38:03.160
that that level of authenticity,

00:38:03.160 --> 00:38:04.860
just in terms of bringing
that part to life

00:38:04.860 --> 00:38:07.937
was something that
really struck me as well.

00:38:07.937 --> 00:38:10.740
And then again, as a mental
health advocate, just again,

00:38:10.740 --> 00:38:13.540
getting back to those awkward
or uncomfortable silences,

00:38:13.540 --> 00:38:15.027
the ways that you've all highlighted that,

00:38:15.027 --> 00:38:16.230
and that's probably that urge

00:38:16.230 --> 00:38:17.990
I was having to kind of
push things forward as well,

00:38:17.990 --> 00:38:20.240
just because, we've talked about that

00:38:20.240 --> 00:38:22.300
in a lot of different communities,

00:38:22.300 --> 00:38:26.020
even in African American
and black communities

00:38:26.020 --> 00:38:27.270
and others immigrant communities,

00:38:27.270 --> 00:38:29.850
as you all have mentioned them
also, the son of immigrants,

00:38:29.850 --> 00:38:33.170
but the ways that those things
can be attention and sit.

00:38:33.170 --> 00:38:34.260
So I'm wondering if any of you

00:38:34.260 --> 00:38:36.310
would be willing to just share

00:38:36.310 --> 00:38:38.070
what you've observed either

00:38:38.070 --> 00:38:42.640
within Cambodian American
communities in terms of

00:38:42.640 --> 00:38:43.490
these awkward sciences.

00:38:43.490 --> 00:38:46.050
Are things moving forward
because in the play,

00:38:46.050 --> 00:38:47.420
that was definitely something I took away

00:38:47.420 --> 00:38:48.770
that you're trying to
move these things for

00:38:48.770 --> 00:38:51.460
and that did happen in the
play in terms of talking about

00:38:51.460 --> 00:38:53.280
some of the mental health
challenges and traumas,

00:38:53.280 --> 00:38:55.610
but anything you all are
willing to share from

00:38:55.610 --> 00:38:58.980
either your experiences or
observations in the communities

00:38:58.980 --> 00:38:59.813
that you've been involved

00:38:59.813 --> 00:39:01.700
in terms of mental health conversations.

00:39:03.230 --> 00:39:04.860
- First I wanna say

00:39:04.860 --> 00:39:07.660
I am not a member of the
Cambodian American community.

00:39:07.660 --> 00:39:10.030
I am of Korean heritage,

00:39:10.030 --> 00:39:13.470
but I was raised by the Browns

00:39:13.470 --> 00:39:16.730
who are a very white couple
from upstate New York.

00:39:16.730 --> 00:39:21.730
So I cannot speak to that question.

00:39:22.320 --> 00:39:24.183
I can say that as an actress,

00:39:26.280 --> 00:39:30.410
therapy is not a foreign island at all

00:39:30.410 --> 00:39:32.810
because most actors are troubled enough

00:39:32.810 --> 00:39:36.280
where they need to a
good amount of therapy

00:39:36.280 --> 00:39:37.970
just to be able to access themselves

00:39:37.970 --> 00:39:40.400
and know what's going on
in their emotional lives.

00:39:40.400 --> 00:39:45.400
So like I understand the
benefit of being to go through

00:39:48.360 --> 00:39:53.257
your own personal ghosts and horrors

00:39:54.463 --> 00:39:58.330
and to bring it out as an
actress that actually...

00:39:59.580 --> 00:40:02.050
I don't think it would be the idea

00:40:02.050 --> 00:40:04.510
of erasing people's
memories I feel like is

00:40:06.800 --> 00:40:08.090
a take away from that person.

00:40:08.090 --> 00:40:10.890
Like Durani says in the play

00:40:10.890 --> 00:40:13.046
that you change that person,

00:40:13.046 --> 00:40:15.130
you don't wanna erase everything there

00:40:15.130 --> 00:40:17.040
because it could affect them in a way

00:40:17.040 --> 00:40:19.003
where they may not be recognizable.

00:40:20.300 --> 00:40:25.300
And I believe that, even
though if it keeps you

00:40:25.670 --> 00:40:29.040
from being able to live your life at all,

00:40:29.040 --> 00:40:30.240
where you can't get outta bed

00:40:30.240 --> 00:40:32.250
and you just wanna die
all the time, then yeah,

00:40:32.250 --> 00:40:34.670
maybe it would have some sort of benefit,

00:40:34.670 --> 00:40:39.023
but for most people, I feel
like you need your history,

00:40:40.070 --> 00:40:42.360
but for the rest of the community,

00:40:42.360 --> 00:40:44.100
I can't speak to that at all.

00:40:44.100 --> 00:40:45.900
That would have to be Robert to say.

00:40:46.880 --> 00:40:48.883
- I appreciate you sharing
that perspective as well.

00:40:48.883 --> 00:40:51.420
I agree with you and then
we've talked about this

00:40:51.420 --> 00:40:53.650
on the podcast, but the learning

00:40:53.650 --> 00:40:56.210
that can happen from painful
and challenging situations

00:40:56.210 --> 00:40:58.660
that you wouldn't want to
lose and remove as well

00:40:59.755 --> 00:41:01.883
even though there still
are challenging people.

00:41:06.032 --> 00:41:10.365
- I guess I'll say the
Cambodian American experience

00:41:11.540 --> 00:41:15.170
in the community in my mind,

00:41:15.170 --> 00:41:20.170
in my heart and also the
mental illness as its own thing

00:41:20.370 --> 00:41:24.543
are very kind of interwoven
in my experience.

00:41:27.750 --> 00:41:29.770
And that just is,

00:41:29.770 --> 00:41:32.280
I don't know if it's good or bad

00:41:32.280 --> 00:41:34.180
or there's so many
conversations to have with that,

00:41:34.180 --> 00:41:36.813
but that's how it has been for me.

00:41:39.620 --> 00:41:42.300
And Pisay is reading this book,

00:41:42.300 --> 00:41:45.423
which I really love, like
when I first read that book,

00:41:46.270 --> 00:41:49.090
it changed a lot of my ideas.

00:41:49.090 --> 00:41:53.360
I saw things very differently after that.

00:41:53.360 --> 00:41:58.230
And I think, man, I don't know,

00:41:58.230 --> 00:42:00.340
I have very strong opinions I think

00:42:00.340 --> 00:42:01.893
about when it comes to the conversations

00:42:01.893 --> 00:42:03.350
that we have about mental health

00:42:03.350 --> 00:42:04.430
and I think

00:42:04.430 --> 00:42:06.820
we're definitely having
more conversations today,

00:42:06.820 --> 00:42:09.310
but it's one of those
things that feels like

00:42:10.240 --> 00:42:13.010
we're trying to unearth
more of like the realities

00:42:13.010 --> 00:42:15.260
of how we have been having it before.

00:42:15.260 --> 00:42:18.050
There's been so much
damage done I feel like,

00:42:18.050 --> 00:42:21.570
and maybe I'm just so hyper
focused on those things,

00:42:21.570 --> 00:42:26.570
but I've witnessed people
who are close to me

00:42:26.570 --> 00:42:30.633
be heavily kind of like corralled

00:42:32.000 --> 00:42:36.470
within that like big
pharmacy and the larger

00:42:36.470 --> 00:42:39.230
kind of like confirmation
bias of what health is.

00:42:39.230 --> 00:42:41.110
And we know that over time,

00:42:41.110 --> 00:42:43.900
it's been revealed to
be a lot of fallacies

00:42:43.900 --> 00:42:47.073
in terms of health in
general and mental health.

00:42:48.150 --> 00:42:51.260
And sometimes I just get like...

00:42:51.260 --> 00:42:53.060
I'm not sure if it's moving forward,

00:42:53.060 --> 00:42:55.330
even though I ultimately faith wise,

00:42:55.330 --> 00:42:57.260
I do believe that we are moving forward

00:42:57.260 --> 00:42:59.580
with mental health and with the
Cambodian American community

00:42:59.580 --> 00:43:04.560
in terms of how the two
things come together.

00:43:04.560 --> 00:43:06.490
Faith wise, I do believe
we are going forward.

00:43:06.490 --> 00:43:08.760
It just feels like it's one of those like

00:43:09.628 --> 00:43:11.610
two steps forward, one step back.

00:43:11.610 --> 00:43:13.660
And sometimes it's one step
forward, two steps back.

00:43:13.660 --> 00:43:14.950
And I'm just like

00:43:14.950 --> 00:43:17.313
ultimately I do think
we are going forward.

00:43:18.740 --> 00:43:20.147
It's just really difficult.

00:43:21.680 --> 00:43:24.570
I think again with these
marginalized communities

00:43:24.570 --> 00:43:29.570
and also with topics that are
not generally widely at scale

00:43:31.860 --> 00:43:35.380
had this challenges there,

00:43:35.380 --> 00:43:37.300
so because it's important to me

00:43:37.300 --> 00:43:38.660
that doesn't always mean
it's gonna be a point

00:43:38.660 --> 00:43:40.390
to everyone else and it's like

00:43:40.390 --> 00:43:43.600
there's a struggle there with
having that conversation,

00:43:43.600 --> 00:43:45.290
but I'm grateful for this conversation

00:43:45.290 --> 00:43:46.577
cause that's what we're talking about

00:43:46.577 --> 00:43:47.833
and that's what's needed.

00:43:51.560 --> 00:43:52.393
- That's really well said

00:43:52.393 --> 00:43:54.210
and I'm with you on that
a hundred percent too

00:43:54.210 --> 00:43:56.780
and some just thinking

00:43:56.780 --> 00:43:58.810
as you're talking to
and this may be related,

00:43:58.810 --> 00:44:01.240
but also a little bit stuff,
but just like you mentioned,

00:44:01.240 --> 00:44:02.260
a lot of things moving forward,

00:44:02.260 --> 00:44:03.710
but also moving back and the way

00:44:03.710 --> 00:44:06.465
that things are sometimes
portrayed or talked about,

00:44:06.465 --> 00:44:07.830
another thing that we've talked about

00:44:07.830 --> 00:44:09.640
just on this podcast culturally,

00:44:09.640 --> 00:44:13.070
like how are people actually
talking about mental health

00:44:13.070 --> 00:44:14.380
and when we go to different communities,

00:44:14.380 --> 00:44:16.660
are we centering that on
a white male experience?

00:44:16.660 --> 00:44:18.210
Are we actually thinking about the culture

00:44:18.210 --> 00:44:19.740
that people are coming from?

00:44:19.740 --> 00:44:21.620
And that definitely
stood out in the play too

00:44:21.620 --> 00:44:24.350
as you all were acting
out the different roles,

00:44:24.350 --> 00:44:26.960
but like what understanding do people have

00:44:26.960 --> 00:44:30.250
about other people's experience
that cultural competency?

00:44:30.250 --> 00:44:31.400
So I don't know if that's
where you're heading,

00:44:31.400 --> 00:44:32.680
but that's one thing that came to mind

00:44:32.680 --> 00:44:33.513
as you were sharing too,

00:44:33.513 --> 00:44:36.360
which I think makes things go
back and forth sometimes too,

00:44:36.360 --> 00:44:38.280
because you can actually
end up making things worse

00:44:38.280 --> 00:44:40.250
because you're approaching
it in one framework.

00:44:40.250 --> 00:44:42.340
There's a real important topic to discuss

00:44:42.340 --> 00:44:44.670
within mental health, but you approach it

00:44:44.670 --> 00:44:47.530
from the wrong angle that
you actually do more harm

00:44:47.530 --> 00:44:50.140
so I think those are really
important conversation.

00:44:50.140 --> 00:44:52.160
And again, for me, it's
encouraging just to be all...

00:44:52.160 --> 00:44:54.110
Robert, you mentioned to be
able to have this conversation

00:44:54.110 --> 00:44:57.620
in this space to continue
to elevate those pieces

00:44:57.620 --> 00:44:59.210
and to make sure that
things are done in a way

00:44:59.210 --> 00:45:02.069
that's appropriate and that
there's room for learning

00:45:02.069 --> 00:45:04.530
and that no one says they
have kind of all the answers

00:45:04.530 --> 00:45:05.700
from one framework alone.

00:45:05.700 --> 00:45:06.780
So as you can tell,

00:45:06.780 --> 00:45:09.073
that definitely resonates with me as well.

00:45:10.890 --> 00:45:13.490
Pisay, anything that you
want to add on that topic

00:45:13.490 --> 00:45:14.340
as we're talking.

00:45:15.300 --> 00:45:17.700
- A couple of things that
came to mind for me was like,

00:45:17.700 --> 00:45:22.500
well I didn't wanna answer that
question because I was like,

00:45:22.500 --> 00:45:25.720
well I don't want people
to think that my experience

00:45:25.720 --> 00:45:28.323
is the Cambodian American experience.

00:45:32.090 --> 00:45:36.070
So my parents really were
open to talking about it,

00:45:36.070 --> 00:45:37.520
talked about it all the time.

00:45:39.730 --> 00:45:42.230
I just didn't listen when I was a kid

00:45:43.830 --> 00:45:46.880
or they talked about it in different ways.

00:45:46.880 --> 00:45:49.560
It was like, you must
speak Cambodian at home.

00:45:49.560 --> 00:45:51.680
You're not allowed to speak English

00:45:51.680 --> 00:45:54.280
because we had so much erase

00:45:54.280 --> 00:45:57.070
or you have to learn how
to do Cambodian ballet

00:45:57.070 --> 00:45:58.530
or listen to Cambodian music

00:45:58.530 --> 00:46:01.200
so it felt like it was very forced upon.

00:46:01.200 --> 00:46:03.500
But then of course I hear people

00:46:03.500 --> 00:46:05.433
whose parents never spoke about it.

00:46:06.450 --> 00:46:11.240
I was talking to a mixed
Cambodian American friend

00:46:11.240 --> 00:46:13.200
who didn't know who...

00:46:13.200 --> 00:46:14.290
I said (speaking foreign language),

00:46:14.290 --> 00:46:17.480
which is a translation
for the Khmer Rouge.

00:46:17.480 --> 00:46:18.860
And he didn't know who that was

00:46:18.860 --> 00:46:23.680
and I was like blown away
that he at least didn't know

00:46:23.680 --> 00:46:25.120
what that word was

00:46:25.120 --> 00:46:27.920
cause I heard it all the time growing up.

00:46:27.920 --> 00:46:31.010
And then I remember hearing
about someone's relative

00:46:31.010 --> 00:46:34.730
who was homeless because
they had some mental issues

00:46:34.730 --> 00:46:36.644
and it shocked me.

00:46:36.644 --> 00:46:39.770
It was like I can't imagine anybody

00:46:39.770 --> 00:46:44.020
in the Cambodian community
being homeless because

00:46:44.020 --> 00:46:46.290
we always take care of
our own, it was the idea.

00:46:46.290 --> 00:46:49.050
So that's just all to say

00:46:50.090 --> 00:46:54.713
that there is just such a
wide range of experiences.

00:46:55.770 --> 00:47:00.770
And also based upon I'm
certain which wave you came in,

00:47:01.230 --> 00:47:04.020
that was something that
was really new to me

00:47:04.020 --> 00:47:08.070
when I started doing the
play is realizing, oh,

00:47:08.070 --> 00:47:10.930
that's why some Cambodians
might be different from me

00:47:10.930 --> 00:47:13.100
or others is depending on the wave.

00:47:13.100 --> 00:47:16.180
So were you the first wave that got out

00:47:17.740 --> 00:47:20.960
with the American troops escaping Cambodia

00:47:20.960 --> 00:47:23.420
because you had the money or even prior?

00:47:23.420 --> 00:47:25.240
Were you the second wave,

00:47:25.240 --> 00:47:27.720
which I can't remember
quite when that was,

00:47:27.720 --> 00:47:30.010
maybe that was right
when things were falling

00:47:30.010 --> 00:47:33.590
and the third wave, which is
what my family went through,

00:47:33.590 --> 00:47:35.900
being refugees, being in Thailand

00:47:35.900 --> 00:47:37.600
after the country had fallen.

00:47:37.600 --> 00:47:41.630
So I'm certain that your mental health

00:47:41.630 --> 00:47:44.083
and experience is also based on that.

00:47:45.020 --> 00:47:49.120
Another thing that came up
for me was my family and I

00:47:49.970 --> 00:47:53.430
were blessed enough to go
back to Cambodia together.

00:47:53.430 --> 00:47:56.610
I had gone there with just
my mom and then a friend

00:47:56.610 --> 00:48:00.307
and then my brother who was
born in the camps with me

00:48:00.307 --> 00:48:04.260
and my sister who was born in
America, we all went together.

00:48:04.260 --> 00:48:07.693
So there are these like three
very different experiences,

00:48:09.130 --> 00:48:12.540
but what really struck my brother

00:48:14.300 --> 00:48:16.310
was how happy everyone was.

00:48:16.310 --> 00:48:20.290
And he's a very happy person,
but he couldn't believe

00:48:22.110 --> 00:48:24.493
you're going to a third world country.

00:48:25.520 --> 00:48:29.480
There's people who don't have
nearly as much materialism

00:48:29.480 --> 00:48:32.280
as you do, electricity,
running water and he was like,

00:48:32.280 --> 00:48:35.340
people here are so happy.

00:48:35.340 --> 00:48:37.600
It was what his experience was.

00:48:37.600 --> 00:48:42.600
And then learning that there
isn't a word for depression.

00:48:43.100 --> 00:48:44.010
I mean, I don't know the word.

00:48:44.010 --> 00:48:46.170
Robert, if you know the word
for depression in Khmer,

00:48:46.170 --> 00:48:49.500
I'm happy to hear it to be educated,

00:48:49.500 --> 00:48:52.610
but there isn't even a word for that.

00:48:52.610 --> 00:48:57.190
So how do you begin to have a
conversation about something

00:48:57.190 --> 00:49:01.690
that, again, going back to
like verbalizing the thing

00:49:01.690 --> 00:49:04.570
in which you're experiencing
is so important,

00:49:04.570 --> 00:49:06.980
how do you begin to have that
discussion when you don't even

00:49:06.980 --> 00:49:08.530
have a word for the thing

00:49:08.530 --> 00:49:11.590
that you're supposed to
try to be verbalizing?

00:49:11.590 --> 00:49:13.883
So not an easy answer.

00:49:19.334 --> 00:49:20.290
- And I appreciate you highlighting

00:49:20.290 --> 00:49:22.090
those complexities as well,

00:49:22.090 --> 00:49:24.320
because it's not a monolith experience,

00:49:24.320 --> 00:49:25.153
like you mentioned,

00:49:25.153 --> 00:49:27.530
and just the different pieces
that you've even started

00:49:27.530 --> 00:49:28.720
to see more and more over time,

00:49:28.720 --> 00:49:31.250
which I know is gonna be helpful
for all of us to continue

00:49:31.250 --> 00:49:33.365
to think about so I appreciate you.

00:49:33.365 --> 00:49:34.340
And just sharing your experiences,

00:49:34.340 --> 00:49:37.470
going back as well with your
family and your brother.

00:49:37.470 --> 00:49:38.320
I mean, there's so many things

00:49:38.320 --> 00:49:40.270
we could actually unpack there as well,

00:49:40.270 --> 00:49:42.173
but definitely appreciate that.

00:49:43.560 --> 00:49:45.330
- Yeah, of course.

00:49:46.310 --> 00:49:48.970
- So if we start to wrap up
this time has gone quickly, Sam,

00:49:48.970 --> 00:49:52.850
I want to loop back to you
and also just talk about

00:49:52.850 --> 00:49:56.020
on here what you want audience
members to actually take

00:49:56.020 --> 00:49:58.900
from the production as they're watching it

00:49:58.900 --> 00:50:01.703
and experiencing it, and then
we'll ask the others as well.

00:50:02.880 --> 00:50:03.990
- That's always a hard question.

00:50:03.990 --> 00:50:06.450
I feel like everyone
comes to it so different

00:50:06.450 --> 00:50:07.990
with their own set of experiences

00:50:07.990 --> 00:50:10.710
and like different elements of the play

00:50:10.710 --> 00:50:14.210
will resonate differently for everyone.

00:50:14.210 --> 00:50:16.773
And I don't mean that to
not answer the question,

00:50:16.773 --> 00:50:17.910
I just think that I don't have an idea

00:50:17.910 --> 00:50:19.870
of like what I want
people to take from it.

00:50:19.870 --> 00:50:23.720
I just hope that people connect with it

00:50:23.720 --> 00:50:26.830
in some way that it illuminates
something for them or

00:50:29.210 --> 00:50:33.900
allows that people can respond

00:50:33.900 --> 00:50:38.210
and like feel some connection
with the material in a way

00:50:38.210 --> 00:50:41.210
that you can take like reframe something

00:50:41.210 --> 00:50:43.590
or it allows you to maybe
experience something

00:50:44.890 --> 00:50:45.850
a little bit differently

00:50:45.850 --> 00:50:50.560
or see something a little bit differently

00:50:50.560 --> 00:50:52.180
or reinforce something.

00:50:52.180 --> 00:50:55.940
I mean, even if someone
pushes back against something

00:50:55.940 --> 00:51:00.940
very strongly and I think
that's engagement and for me,

00:51:01.250 --> 00:51:02.980
it's about connecting with people

00:51:04.760 --> 00:51:06.700
and whatever way people come to the story

00:51:06.700 --> 00:51:09.370
and whatever way they connect with it is

00:51:09.370 --> 00:51:12.033
how that person will connect.

00:51:13.340 --> 00:51:16.440
So that's how I feel.

00:51:16.440 --> 00:51:17.780
- Really well said.

00:51:17.780 --> 00:51:19.770
That was a good answer to your question.

00:51:19.770 --> 00:51:20.950
Not avoid it, but I think

00:51:20.950 --> 00:51:23.464
that's really, really, really important

00:51:23.464 --> 00:51:24.790
the way you phrased that.

00:51:24.790 --> 00:51:25.760
Robert, what about you?

00:51:25.760 --> 00:51:27.140
Maybe I should rephrase the question

00:51:27.140 --> 00:51:28.870
so not necessarily that
people would take from it,

00:51:28.870 --> 00:51:30.780
but while you're also hoping to...

00:51:30.780 --> 00:51:33.990
Maybe just the hope that you have for us

00:51:33.990 --> 00:51:35.900
as a society moving forward,

00:51:35.900 --> 00:51:36.733
just either personally

00:51:36.733 --> 00:51:39.253
or within the context of the production.

00:51:42.050 --> 00:51:47.000
- I think my hopes have been very focused

00:51:48.060 --> 00:51:49.650
throughout the entire
thing and still is now

00:51:49.650 --> 00:51:51.443
is that it's very simple.

00:51:52.600 --> 00:51:56.870
I'm only hoping that
Cambodian people see it

00:51:56.870 --> 00:52:01.870
and can have a growing
sensation or a growing feeling

00:52:03.610 --> 00:52:08.610
of their own selves, of
their own importance,

00:52:09.080 --> 00:52:10.680
of their own place in the world.

00:52:11.810 --> 00:52:13.710
And that has been kind
of like my only concern.

00:52:13.710 --> 00:52:15.430
Like to be very honest,

00:52:15.430 --> 00:52:18.380
I'm not really concerned with
anyone else seeing it at all.

00:52:19.580 --> 00:52:23.050
And it's just like I get really...

00:52:25.900 --> 00:52:28.260
I get really so many mixed emotions,

00:52:28.260 --> 00:52:30.400
but I do get very happy

00:52:30.400 --> 00:52:32.300
to think about someone
having that experience

00:52:32.300 --> 00:52:34.930
and because I know some of
the experiences I've had

00:52:34.930 --> 00:52:37.777
and the struggles I've had to
kind of like come into myself

00:52:37.777 --> 00:52:39.440
and to allow myself to be like

00:52:39.440 --> 00:52:42.010
this is something I deserve,

00:52:42.010 --> 00:52:46.120
or this is something that is
like I'm worth this or that,

00:52:46.120 --> 00:52:48.960
and if there's anyone out there
who has similar experiences

00:52:48.960 --> 00:52:51.679
to me who feels that
way, I relate to that.

00:52:51.679 --> 00:52:52.512
And I think it can be very important

00:52:52.512 --> 00:52:54.440
for that person to have that experience,

00:52:54.440 --> 00:52:59.440
to sit there and essentially
be talked about for two hours.

00:52:59.550 --> 00:53:02.130
And eventually you start
to get that feeling,

00:53:02.130 --> 00:53:03.890
is that like if I sit
a hundred people down

00:53:03.890 --> 00:53:05.920
and I talk about you for two hours,

00:53:05.920 --> 00:53:07.110
eventually you're gonna be like, oh,

00:53:07.110 --> 00:53:11.810
maybe I'm important cause
people are like staying on it.

00:53:11.810 --> 00:53:13.180
So that's my hope, that's my hope

00:53:13.180 --> 00:53:16.410
and I don't know how far I can go.

00:53:16.410 --> 00:53:19.340
I don't know all the different
ways that it can be received

00:53:19.340 --> 00:53:23.060
or all the different
responses, but that is my hope.

00:53:24.470 --> 00:53:25.303
- I appreciate that.

00:53:25.303 --> 00:53:28.220
Being seen, being seen and elevated

00:53:28.220 --> 00:53:29.300
in lots of different ways.

00:53:29.300 --> 00:53:30.197
Thanks so much for sharing that.

00:53:30.197 --> 00:53:32.747
And I can definitely tell
that passion's there too.

00:53:35.630 --> 00:53:36.780
Sonnie, what about you?

00:53:41.860 --> 00:53:43.180
- What I hope people take away from...

00:53:43.180 --> 00:53:46.810
Well, I hope not just
Cambodian Americans see this.

00:53:46.810 --> 00:53:48.430
I hope everyone sees

00:53:49.860 --> 00:53:53.050
because I feel like there
are universal truths

00:53:53.050 --> 00:53:56.080
being told in the show.

00:53:56.080 --> 00:53:59.710
And so I feel like it can
resonate with a lot of people.

00:53:59.710 --> 00:54:01.460
More than one mother has
come up to me and said,

00:54:01.460 --> 00:54:03.510
I know that relationship
between mother and daughter.

00:54:03.510 --> 00:54:05.663
I was like, cool.

00:54:06.800 --> 00:54:08.962
That you can recognize that something

00:54:08.962 --> 00:54:13.053
that we've brought to life on stage.

00:54:14.330 --> 00:54:17.150
I would like people to
understand the truth

00:54:18.530 --> 00:54:23.530
that's being told, that
America is not the big hero

00:54:23.580 --> 00:54:25.540
that everyone wants them to believe.

00:54:25.540 --> 00:54:27.600
We're a great country,

00:54:27.600 --> 00:54:31.283
but we have flaws and we have
done some heinous things.

00:54:32.400 --> 00:54:35.690
And I think shedding a light on that

00:54:35.690 --> 00:54:37.990
cause a lot of people only know Vietnam.

00:54:37.990 --> 00:54:40.230
A lot of people don't understand full pot,

00:54:40.230 --> 00:54:42.630
and what happened in
Cambodia and that's something

00:54:42.630 --> 00:54:44.580
that should be taught in history alongside

00:54:44.580 --> 00:54:46.433
with a lot of other stuff.

00:54:48.560 --> 00:54:52.520
A lot of people are trying to whitewash,

00:54:52.520 --> 00:54:54.083
if that's the right word.

00:54:55.030 --> 00:54:58.140
Right now, it's going on,
censoring is going on.

00:54:58.140 --> 00:55:02.630
And I feel like it's important
now to tell the truths

00:55:02.630 --> 00:55:06.010
of whether it puts you
in a bad light or not.

00:55:06.010 --> 00:55:08.290
The truth is more important because

00:55:08.290 --> 00:55:11.840
hopefully it will be repeated historically

00:55:13.900 --> 00:55:16.003
like it is being done today.

00:55:16.870 --> 00:55:19.670
So yeah, I feel like it's
important in that way

00:55:19.670 --> 00:55:21.070
that everyone should see it.

00:55:22.070 --> 00:55:23.020
- Really well said.

00:55:24.790 --> 00:55:26.840
Pisay, I'm gonna give you the final word.

00:55:32.240 --> 00:55:33.350
- Well, just to say,

00:55:33.350 --> 00:55:36.290
I should definitely not
have the final word.

00:55:36.290 --> 00:55:37.700
After this is all said and done

00:55:37.700 --> 00:55:40.000
and people listen to the podcast,

00:55:40.000 --> 00:55:44.520
I do hope that it encourages
people to do their own research

00:55:44.520 --> 00:55:46.300
and come to their own conclusions

00:55:47.860 --> 00:55:49.840
about things that they may
not have been familiar with

00:55:49.840 --> 00:55:51.290
or things that interest them,

00:55:52.535 --> 00:55:55.423
things that they may disagree
with, whatever it may be.

00:55:56.870 --> 00:55:57.990
I just know that

00:55:57.990 --> 00:55:59.420
when I get really interested in something,

00:55:59.420 --> 00:56:02.010
I'll start Googling it,
I'll start looking it up.

00:56:02.010 --> 00:56:03.290
So I think that's the important thing

00:56:03.290 --> 00:56:06.003
is that we somehow peak
someone's interest out there.

00:56:07.004 --> 00:56:09.683
My goal is twofold.

00:56:12.753 --> 00:56:14.600
There's so much information in the play,

00:56:14.600 --> 00:56:15.940
so much good information,

00:56:15.940 --> 00:56:18.140
information that I didn't even know.

00:56:18.140 --> 00:56:22.563
So any information that
people can take from the play,

00:56:23.730 --> 00:56:25.620
whether it's related to science,

00:56:25.620 --> 00:56:27.570
whether it's related to the brain

00:56:27.570 --> 00:56:29.420
and relationships and fear memory,

00:56:29.420 --> 00:56:33.090
or whether it's related
to Cambodian stories

00:56:33.090 --> 00:56:35.560
cause that's also really,
really rich there too.

00:56:35.560 --> 00:56:38.260
Sam did such a great job of incorporating

00:56:38.260 --> 00:56:43.090
so many experiences and
ideas and histories.

00:56:43.090 --> 00:56:44.930
So anything that people can take away

00:56:44.930 --> 00:56:47.180
from that aspect and say, oh,

00:56:47.180 --> 00:56:49.600
I didn't know that, I had no idea,

00:56:49.600 --> 00:56:52.780
that makes me happy to hear my friends

00:56:52.780 --> 00:56:53.970
or whoever come to the play and say,

00:56:53.970 --> 00:56:57.963
I didn't realize that
because that's the seed.

00:56:59.880 --> 00:57:01.470
And then the second thing is

00:57:01.470 --> 00:57:06.110
having worked on Pia for so long,

00:57:06.110 --> 00:57:08.840
researching different
aspects of neuroscience

00:57:08.840 --> 00:57:11.120
and really thinking about
where she comes from

00:57:11.120 --> 00:57:13.650
and her history and her story,

00:57:13.650 --> 00:57:17.320
I can't help but feel
like a kinship towards her

00:57:17.320 --> 00:57:19.320
and really wanna advocate

00:57:19.320 --> 00:57:21.460
and be protective of the fact that

00:57:22.680 --> 00:57:27.080
I hope people can walk
away understanding that

00:57:27.950 --> 00:57:31.230
her blind spot was her love for her family

00:57:31.230 --> 00:57:36.230
and her wanting a need
to have this connection.

00:57:36.670 --> 00:57:40.460
She's a lot of like very angry
outbursts and a lot of...

00:57:42.462 --> 00:57:44.220
She has a lot of things that maybe

00:57:44.220 --> 00:57:47.380
didn't even make sense to me
sometimes so I think that,

00:57:47.380 --> 00:57:52.380
I hope that parents and children walk away

00:57:53.170 --> 00:57:55.720
sort of understanding like, oh,

00:57:55.720 --> 00:57:59.957
is that something that I do
or have experienced myself?

00:58:00.940 --> 00:58:04.900
I did have one friend who
couldn't speak after the play.

00:58:04.900 --> 00:58:08.440
She was so emotional
about her own relationship

00:58:08.440 --> 00:58:11.600
with her kids because she
had seen the relationship

00:58:11.600 --> 00:58:14.580
that Pia and Shantrea had.

00:58:14.580 --> 00:58:19.390
So in some ways, it's
like, yes, we did it.

00:58:21.600 --> 00:58:24.100
But yeah, those are my two goals.

00:58:24.100 --> 00:58:24.933
- That's great.

00:58:26.480 --> 00:58:28.510
Well, again, I appreciate
what you all have done

00:58:28.510 --> 00:58:30.820
in this production from
the writing to the acting

00:58:30.820 --> 00:58:32.900
to so many different components and again,

00:58:32.900 --> 00:58:35.760
just how you all engaged
in this conversation too

00:58:35.760 --> 00:58:37.530
cause some of these
topics are emotional too.

00:58:37.530 --> 00:58:39.770
I know we were entering in together

00:58:39.770 --> 00:58:42.170
so I definitely appreciate
your willingness to do that.

00:58:42.170 --> 00:58:43.643
And you can tell that this is something

00:58:43.643 --> 00:58:45.330
that I've been really excited about

00:58:45.330 --> 00:58:46.530
so I also appreciate your patience

00:58:46.530 --> 00:58:48.270
with my five questions in the row

00:58:48.270 --> 00:58:51.200
cause they're just kind of
all bubbling out at once.

00:58:51.200 --> 00:58:53.430
But again, really grateful
for the production.

00:58:53.430 --> 00:58:54.320
I hope folks,

00:58:54.320 --> 00:58:56.307
this will be airing in
time for folks to still go

00:58:56.307 --> 00:58:58.407
and see the production if they're able to.

00:58:59.620 --> 00:59:00.900
And again, all the
things that you all share

00:59:00.900 --> 00:59:04.610
just in terms of the impact
it's gonna have for Cambodians

00:59:04.610 --> 00:59:05.960
and for society in general.

00:59:05.960 --> 00:59:09.210
So, grateful for the time you
all have taken to talk here

00:59:09.210 --> 00:59:10.043
on the podcast.

00:59:10.043 --> 00:59:12.360
All the listeners will enjoy hearing this

00:59:12.360 --> 00:59:14.770
and I imagine that people will
do some research afterwards

00:59:14.770 --> 00:59:17.350
and even go back and
re-listen to the episode

00:59:17.350 --> 00:59:20.040
cause there's so many nuggets
along the way as well.

00:59:20.040 --> 00:59:21.560
So appreciate you all being here

00:59:21.560 --> 00:59:23.753
and thanks so much for
joining the Addy Hour.

00:59:24.620 --> 00:59:26.060
- Thank you Doctor.

00:59:26.060 --> 00:59:27.115
- Thank you.

00:59:27.115 --> 00:59:28.615
- Thanks so much.

00:59:29.502 --> 00:59:32.085
(upbeat music)

